\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

\looseness=-1 Frozen flow-matching Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models offer a compelling deployment paradigm: a single pretrained policy can handle diverse manipulation tasks without retraining~\cite{black2024pi0,shukor2025smolvla,lipman2023flow}. Yet their action quality is bounded by the pretrained weights, and fine-tuning remains expensive when new tasks arrive frequently. Post-hoc learned correction---a lightweight module that refines actions at inference time without modifying VLA weights~\cite{snell2024scaling}---is an appealing alternative. But does it actually help?

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig1_pipeline.pdf}
  \caption{Calibrated gating pipeline. $K{=}2$ action samples are drawn from the frozen VLA; their pairwise $L_2$ distance forms a calibrated uncertainty score. When the score falls below $\tau$ (confident step), the argmin-to-centroid sample is output directly. When it exceeds $\tau$ (uncertain step), a lightweight CRM applies bounded correction ($\|\boldsymbol{\delta}\|_2 \leq \varepsilon$).}
  \label{fig:pipeline}
\end{figure}

\looseness=-1 We systematically evaluate this idea and find a surprising negative result: unconditional learned correction is universally harmful. Across four LIBERO manipulation suites~\cite{liu2023libero} and two VLAs spanning an order of magnitude in scale, applying a correction module at every control step consistently degrades performance (Section~\ref{sec:failure_modes}). The problem is not the correction module itself, but the assumption that every step needs correction.

\looseness=-1 Recent work addresses parts of this problem space without confronting the core failure mode. A2C2~\cite{a2c2_2025} learns a correction head on action chunks but applies it unconditionally and targets temporal staleness rather than initial action quality. ReconVLA~\cite{chen2026reconvla} quantifies uncertainty over frozen VLAs but does not learn a corrective residual. SC-VLA~\cite{liu2026scvla} uses reinforcement learning and therefore abandons the weight-frozen constraint. VLA-Pilot~\cite{li2025vlapilot} performs evolutionary search at $2.41$\,s per control step, too slow for closed-loop deployment. None analyze when correction helps versus hurts, nor provide a mechanism to suppress correction when the VLA is already confident.

\looseness=-1 Our analysis reveals the root cause: the vast majority of control steps already produce confident, low-variance actions, and corrections on these steps inject noise rather than signal. This has a direct consequence: knowing \emph{when not to correct} matters more than the correction's magnitude. We propose a simple gating mechanism: two stochastic action samples drawn in a single batched forward pass provide a per-step uncertainty estimate at zero extra latency. A calibrated threshold on their pairwise distance gates correction to the small fraction of genuinely uncertain steps, while the VLA's own output passes through on confident steps. This selective prediction approach~\cite{chow1970optimum,geifman2017selective} restricts correction to the 3--20\% of steps where it is most needed.

\looseness=-1 On SmolVLA~\cite{shukor2025smolvla} (0.4B) across four LIBERO suites, selective gating recovers nearly all degradation from unconditional correction. Cross-VLA experiments on $\pi_{0.5}$~\cite{black2025pi05} (3.6B, 94--98\% baseline) provide preliminary evidence that the failure pattern persists and amplifies on stronger models. Threshold sensitivity analysis shows that gating is robust to $\tau$ choice, and trigger rate correlates monotonically with effect size across suites (Appendix~\ref{sec:tau_sweep}).

Our contributions are:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,nosep]
  \item \textbf{Failure analysis of unconditional correction} (Section~\ref{sec:failure_modes}): we show that applying learned correction at every step degrades frozen VLAs across all evaluated task suites and model scales, and identify confident steps---where the VLA needs no help---as the source of harm.
  \item \textbf{Calibrated gating via pairwise uncertainty} (Section~\ref{sec:method}): two action samples yield a per-step uncertainty proxy that gates correction to the small fraction of uncertain steps, recovering nearly all degradation at zero extra latency.
  \item \textbf{Cross-suite and cross-VLA validation} (Section~\ref{sec:experiments}; Appendix~\ref{sec:cross_vla}): trigger rate and recovery show monotonic correlation across suites, and the degradation pattern generalizes from SmolVLA (0.4B) to $\pi_{0.5}$ (3.6B), providing evidence that the mechanism is not architecture-specific.
\end{enumerate}

